  "slug": "bacterial-computers-a611",
  "summary": "Terabyte-scale metagenomic data and large prokaryotic datasets make scalability and computational efficiency central to genomic analysis tools.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these three preprints is scalability in genomic analysis: each tool addresses a computational bottleneck in extracting interpretable units from sequence data. PPanGGOLiN V2 extends a graph-based, statistical framework for prokaryotic pangenomes with new analyses, a redesigned architecture, and faster performance on large datasets. PlasChain builds on SCAPP's cycle-peeling by adding contig paths and cycle merging, aiming to recover long plasmids from short-read metagenome assemblies. vOMIX-MEGA targets end-to-end viral identification at terabyte scale, where scalability and computational resources are stated as limiting factors. The methods are distinct and the items do not share a single algorithm; what is new is the combination of graph-based reconstruction and pipeline-level engineering to handle larger inputs.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-07-31"
}
